<p>
  Earthing designs that work well in a wet climate often struggle in the Gulf, the Sahel and other arid regions. Dry sand, gravel and rock are poor conductors, and an electrode that measured well in one season can read far higher in another. This guide explains why, how to measure soil properly before you design, and what to do when the ground simply will not cooperate.
</p>

<h2 id="why-high">Why Dry Ground Gives High Resistance</h2>

<p>
  Soil conducts mainly through moisture and the dissolved salts in it, not through the mineral grains themselves. Dry sand and rock hold almost no water, so their resistivity is very high. Temperature matters too: as soil approaches freezing or dries in extreme heat, resistivity rises. Coastal flats and salt-affected ground are a different problem, because they conduct well but are strongly corrosive to copper and steel electrodes, so the electrode material has to be chosen with that in mind.
</p>

<h2 id="measure-resistivity">Measure Soil Resistivity Before You Design</h2>

<p>
  Do not guess. Resistivity is measured with the four-pin Wenner method: four electrodes driven in a straight line at equal spacing <em>a</em>, with the instrument reading a resistance <em>R</em>. Soil resistivity is then approximately 2π × a × R. Repeating the measurement at several spacings shows how resistivity changes with depth, which tells you whether to drive rods deeper or spread the electrode wider.
</p>

<Callout type="info" title="Test in the driest conditions you can">
  Resistivity measured just after rain or irrigation is optimistic. Measure at the end of the dry season, or add a seasonal margin, so the electrode still meets its target when the ground is at its worst.
</Callout>

