<p>
  Watering the ground gives a good reading today and a bad one next month. Treat it as a test aid, never as a design solution.
</p>

<h2 id="verify">Verify the Finished Electrode</h2>

<p>
  Once the electrode is installed, measure it with the three-point fall-of-potential method, placing the current and potential spikes far enough from the electrode that their resistance areas do not overlap. In hard or rocky ground it is often difficult to drive spikes, so wet the spike positions lightly to get a stable reading and keep the spacing generous. Our <a href={u('/blog/earth-resistance-testing-guide/')}>earth resistance testing guide</a> walks through the method step by step.
</p>

<h2 id="instrument">Choosing the Instrument</h2>

<p>
  The CIE/222M analog earth tester is available in a four-terminal version that measures both earth resistance and soil resistivity, and the DET-2000 digital earth tester offers the same four-terminal capability with an LCD readout. Both are built and calibrated by CIE. The hand-driven CIE/222M needs no batteries, which is useful at remote sites where charging is not practical. Browse the <a href={u('/products/earth-tester/')}>earth resistance testers</a> or read <a href={u('/blog/digital-vs-analog-earth-tester/')}>digital vs analog earth testers</a> to decide which suits your site.
</p>

